WRINKLE (disclosed honestly, see ATTEMPT.md Sec.5.4 for the full
account): this threshold is much larger than K=3's analogue
(~65 instead of ~3-6). Direct check shows why: this root does NOT
correspond to an x in [0,1] (it is the OTHER real root of -M4's
minimal quartic, +2.8979..., achieved at x=-0.957, outside [0,1] --
a genuine value of the unrestricted-x elimination, correctly
excluded from our domain of interest but not by this computation
alone). The 'no unrestricted-x solution beyond this threshold'
conclusion is still fully valid and sufficient logically (a bigger
domain having no solution implies the [0,1]-restricted domain has
none either) -- it is just not TIGHT. We patch the resulting gap
(n=6.. 64 ) with an exact exhaustive per-integer-n check.

